ing to provide training  to researchers to ensure that they're  able to humanely work with their animals  and it's important to remember that as a  person who works for a laboratory animal  department that you have probably many  more resources and connections than any  individual p I is going to have and you  need to use those connections to help  them as best you can with  you're weird training requests no matter  how strange they are so that might mean  that the trainer gets to go out and  learn something new and bring it home or  it might mean that trainer has to find a  place that the P I can travel to to  learn a procedure or bring a consultant  but any rate you need to make your best  effort to satisfy even the weirdest of  training requests alright continuing on  with our technical topics is anesthesia  and analgesia and if you have enough  staff who need the same kind of training  in anesthesia and analgesia then  offering the class is a great idea the  way we do it is we have a half lecture  half hands-on class it is only geared  towards rodents because that's where our  biggest volume is and if someone is  working with another species then we  handle that as a one-on-one as needed  kind of a request and these are the  topics that we cover in the lecture half  which comes first then we move on to the  hands-on portion and have developed a  good workflow for how to make this part  move quite smoothly we start out by  weighing animals mixing up drugs  calculating and then injecting  anesthetics then we watch the mice very  closely while they fall asleep so that  we can observe them moving through the  different stages of anesthesia also we  can observe when they lose specific  reflexes and monitor parameters to  determine depth of anesthesia once they  have all become anesthetized then I  bring out several I supporting machines  I try and pick some that look a little  different from each other so not just  all of the same and they're completely  disassembled when I  mout so people taking the class can  actually put their hands on the machines  look up the hoses fill them up turn on  the oxygen and learn about how the gas  flows through the machine because I so  flooring training is really as much  about understanding that machine and how  to make the machine work even when you  have some kind of a problem than  anything else so once the machines are  assembled and they anesthetize mice with  ice flooring which gives them a chance  to go through the induction part again  and then also to to watch the recovery  phase and by the time that's finished  some of the animals that we injected  we're given very light doses and they're  already starting to wake up and so then  that's another opportunity to to monitor  anesthesia recovery and I'm going to  lump the next three technical topics  together as surgical training so our  next three items off alistor aseptic  surgical technique surgical training and  pre post procedural care and if you have  sufficient need to put together a class  for this I have a couple of suggestions  about how that might look because we  actually have two different ways that we  offer this the first is actually it's a  credit University class software through  our Animal Sciences Department and it is  comprehensive not only of the three  topics in the title of the slide but  also anesthesia and analgesia it's a  week-long it does include live animal  surgery which in this case is a very  hysterectomy and it's offered every  other time with a different species so  large animal users and small animal  users can choose the more appropriate  species for themselves and take it  either with rats or with sheep the  second method that we're going to be  doing I I'm going to call the everything  but surgery works  up so this will actually not include any  live animals at all it will just be done  with models and include aseptic  technique instruments suturing and  general surgical principles and then as  a follow-up to that workshop then the  trainer will go into the lab with the  researcher with their actual animals and  their actual surgical procedure so one  really nice thing about conducting it in  this way is that the trainer can  actually verify that the researcher took  the information from the workshop and  applied it correctly back to their own  workspace and also a reduction in the  number of animals that that are being  used because we are going to use animals  in just a demonstration all right and  that brings me to the last item on our  list of requirements which is regular  continuing education and for animal care  staff and this is something that can  certainly be done in any way imaginable  it can just be a supervisor walking  around and talking with their staff it  can be field trips it can be seminars it  can be hands-on workshops we do have a  regular siiii event for our staff every  other week here and that seems to work  well and I know a lot of other  facilities are using that same idea I  have just a list here of topics that are  are good ones for seas for animal care  staff I'm sure you guys probably could  add 20 or so more to this list but here  are some ideas and then finally I just  wanted to give you my contact  information and again make the offer  that I'm definitely happy to share with  you any materials that I have anything  that I talked about in the in the  presentation today and thank you very  much  joining me and now I will open up my Q&amp;A  box and see if there's any questions  that I can answer for you right now okay  so how often do you require refresher  training so as I mentioned or I cook  does not require any of hands-on  training specifically so that's I've  never for that I'm its individual  one-on-one basis the basic module that's  the online one showing all the all the  basic items that one's every three years  and the reason that three years was  selected is because we are protocols  could potentially last for three years  so that really is where that time flame  came from next question how much if any  of your course work is available to  anyone online outside of your  institution so none of it the online the  training database only allows you access  if you have a CSU ID so I would be happy  to share with you the powerpoints I  would have to just read acts like the  CSU specific information but they're not  available to the general public alright  next question with euthanasia training  are you utilizing a model for cervical  cervical dislocation or euthanizing an  animal per person per class so for us we  are mice that we use for training are  all donated they're mostly calls from  transgenic breeding colonies that don't  have the right genetics and so they're  going to be euthanized anyway and we  honestly have more than we can use and  so I do use an eyes an animal at least  one animal per person per class rather  than using a model just for the fact  that those animals are already readily  available  they're going to be euthanized anyway so  there there isn't really a need for us  to look for an alternative their next  question how do you respond to pee is  that suggests that the surgical  anesthetic trading is to intensive time  consuming or do you have this complaint  um so as I mentioned are our hands-on  training is not required necessarily by  the I cook and I have found that with  this more voluntary training that people  are always there because they want to be  and not because they're made to be and  so I guess I'm kind of lucky and that I  don't have that I don't have that  complaint where people are complaining  that they have are being forced to take  training they don't want so I don't have  a I don't think i have like a really  good answer to that one all right what  regime of anesthesia do you use in your  classes for mice and rats so the way  that I determined what anesthetics to  focus on in the class was I pulled all  of our protocols and I ran a report on  all the anesthetics that are in there  and i found out that with very very few  exceptions it was a really small list  and I'm so I just focus on those  particular drugs which are ketamine  xylazine acepromazine phenobarbital  dexmedetomidine and I so fine so we talk  will briefly about the fact that there's  all these other drugs out there and chat  with our veterinarians if you need to  find an alternative but in the class we  focus just on the primary ones that we  are actually being used at our  facilities do you train undergraduate  students with your surgical training  program or is this saved for grad and  postdocs it's available to anybody who's  going to be involved with research  surgery and so  definitely does include undergraduate  students although to a lesser extent  next one says wonderful presentation  thank you um do you deal with P is who  are MDS and if you do do they fall the  same and then I don't see the rest of  the question okay so here CSU I don't  deal with MDS very much because we don't  have a medical school we have a  veterinary school in a previous job I  did work with a lot of MDS so I can see  why maybe where this question is coming  from they don't always have as good of  additive attitudes about being turning  them on veterinary procedures but yeah  no we don't we don't have very many MDS  that we're working with here how many  student to trainer ratio detail for your  classes does it depend on the kind of  class so for the hands-on classes I  prefer to have one instructor for every  three students if at all possible  because if you have more students /  instructor than that the class just like  grinds to a halt because there's going  to be people who just stand there until  your next to them holding their hand so  I try very hard to have that three to  one ratio all right I think that is all  of our questions unless some new ones  come in no okay so it looks like that  concludes my presentation today thank  you everyone very much 